2221:LOGBOOK.md:271:- ROOT CAUSE: The r1 matrix assigned `packet-tunnel-provider-systemextension` to all four macOS targets unconditionally while authorizing **Mac Development** profiles for the same four App IDs. Apple DTS is explicit that the value follows the distribution channel: the `-systemextension` suffix is for direct Developer ID signing, and "otherwise — including when you distribute the app on the App Store and when signing for development — use the values without that suffix" (forums/thread/800887). Ceremony C1 would have provisioned development profiles against a value development signing never uses, and C1 is a one-shot human node (ADR-028).
2232:LOGBOOK.md-295-- DECISION: The matrix withholds `keychain-access-groups` from `works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel` (`prohibited-pending-decision`) rather than provisioning a right the provider cannot exercise. Created `TASK-260728-7ii1xz` to select the macOS credential transport and return a grant-or-keep-prohibited amendment; it blocks `379cpk`, `1o9wjz`, `29ws8l`, `3f4lxy`. Five credential tasks all assumed the shared-group model and none owned the question of whether it holds.
2239:LOGBOOK.md:302:- FINDING: Probe identifiers had to be in this matrix, not invented later by `1r0fxv`. C1 authorizes "exactly the approved macOS entries"; had the probe been absent, provisioning it would have needed a second human sitting — the thing ADR-028 forbids.
